#{[17592186366078 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366078 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ 4y+3x\\end{aligned}],$ find  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e24e150-5788-5770-a2a9-706c3d3592ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366078 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot column. Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=0.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-1=2\\cdot 0-1=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366079 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0e35217a-c5a8-5016-8924-bddec15e8b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417]