nd{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404177 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fe978ee-e242-5740-b3cc-178c387af89b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404177 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404178 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e82b63e-4a76-52fe-b5a0-9529b5e4e1a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404179 #edb/ref 1080 "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404179 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860